<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Binary Number Guide</title>

    <style>
        body {
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
            background: #f0f7ff;
            padding: 30px;
        }

        .container {
            max-width: 800px;
            margin: auto;
            background: white;
            padding: 30px;
            border-radius: 15px;
        }

        h1 {
            text-align: center;
            color: #1769aa;
        }

        .box {
            background: #e8f3ff;
            padding: 20px;
            margin: 15px 0;
            border-radius: 10px;
        }

        code {
            background: white;
            padding: 5px 10px;
            border-radius: 5px;
        }
    </style>
</head>

<body>

<div class="container">

    <h1>🔢 Binary Number System</h1>

    <div class="box">
        <h2>What is Binary?</h2>
        <p>Binary is a number system that uses only two digits:</p>
        <p><strong>0 and 1</strong></p>
    </div>

    <div class="box">
        <h2>Why Computers Use Binary?</h2>
        <p>Digital computers work with electronic states that can be represented as 0 and 1.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="box">
        <h2>Example</h2>
        <p>Decimal <strong>5</strong> can be represented in binary as:</p>
        <p><code>101</code></p>
    </div>

    <div class="box">
        <h2>Common Number Systems</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>Binary — Base 2</li>
            <li>Decimal — Base 10</li>
            <li>Octal — Base 8</li>
            <li>Hexadecimal — Base 16</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

</div>

</body>
</html>
